    "lede": "The share of callers who give up before reaching a resolution or a person.",
    "html": "<h2>What is the abandonment rate?</h2><p>The abandonment rate is the share of callers who leave the interaction before reaching a resolution or a person. It is counted against calls that connected rather than against calls that completed, which is what separates it from a satisfaction measure. On a voice line it is usually a timing problem, since callers abandon at the point where waiting stops feeling purposeful.</p><p class=\"gt-alias\"><b>Also called:</b> Call abandonment</p><h2>Why abandonment means nothing until it is located</h2><p>A single abandonment figure for the month tells an operations team almost nothing it can act on. The same percentage describes a queue that loses people during the opening greeting and a queue that loses them while an account lookup is pending, and the remedy for each has nothing in common with the other. What matters is the distribution of abandonment across the call, second by second, against what the agent was doing at that moment. Most voice deployments collect the total and discard the position.</p><ul><li>Abandonment counts the callers an organisation never heard from again.</li><li>The position of the abandonment names the component responsible for it.</li><li>A falling abandonment rate alongside falling resolution means callers are giving up later.</li><li>Outbound and inbound abandonment have different causes and belong in separate reports.</li></ul><h2>How the abandonment rate works</h2><ul><li>Every call is timestamped at connection and at termination, including first agent speech.</li><li>Termination is classified by which party ended the call and at which turn.</li><li>Abandonment is counted against connected calls, with the denominator stated explicitly.</li><li>The distribution of abandonment positions is plotted across the call duration.</li><li>Positions that concentrate abandonment are traced to the step causing the delay.</li><li>The figure is reported per intent, since waiting tolerance differs by task.</li></ul><h2>How the abandonment rate is evaluated</h2><p>Abandonment is only diagnostic once it is located, so the figure is read against the point of departure. Examine the following on connected calls over a full week:</p><ul><li>abandonment rate against connected calls, with the denominator stated</li><li>distribution of abandonment position across the call</li><li>abandonment within the first six seconds of the greeting</li><li>abandonment while a lookup or a transfer is pending</li><li>abandonment compared against the equivalent queue handled by people</li></ul><h2>Limitations, risks and common failure modes</h2><ul><li>Abandonment is frequently reported against offered calls, which flatters the figure.</li><li>A long greeting loses callers before the agent has understood anything.</li><li>Network disconnections are recorded as abandonment, inflating a number nobody can act on.</li><li>Callers who abandon and then redial are counted twice over in the same report.</li></ul>",
