    "slug": "accent-robustness",
    "term": "Accent robustness",
    "set": "evio",
    "txt": "accent robustness whether recognition holds up across the range of accents in a real caller population rather than a test set. conversation evio",
    "def": "Whether recognition holds up across the range of accents in a real caller population rather than a test set.",
    "capability": {
      "label": "Evio",
      "href": "/pyzo/evio/"
    },
    "lede": "Whether recognition holds up across the range of accents in a real caller population rather than a test set.",
    "html": "<h2>What is accent robustness?</h2><p>Accent robustness is whether speech recognition holds its accuracy across the full range of accents present in a real caller population. It is a property of the deployment rather than of the model alone, since the relevant range is whatever the telephone line delivers. A system with strong published accuracy can be unusable in one state and dependable in the next.</p><h2>Why one city of testing is no evidence of accent coverage</h2><p>The usual sequence is a pilot that performs well in the city where the implementation team is located, followed by a national release in which recognition accuracy deteriorates sharply across several states. Vendor accuracy figures are reported on read speech from balanced corpora, where speakers articulate clearly into a good microphone. A real caller population carries regional phonology alongside second language speakers of the interface language, and a mobile network adds its own compression on top of both. None of that appears in a benchmark, so it appears in production instead.</p><ul><li>Published accuracy figures describe read speech rather than telephone conversation.</li><li>Accuracy that varies by region produces service quality that varies by region.</li><li>The accents least represented in training belong to the callers least able to escalate.</li><li>Accent performance can be measured per region, so it never needs to be assumed.</li></ul><h2>How accent robustness works</h2><ul><li>The caller population is described by region and by language background before procurement.</li><li>Audio is sampled from live calls in each region in proportion to volume.</li><li>Recognition accuracy is measured per region rather than as a single aggregate.</li><li>Regions below the acceptable accuracy receive acoustic adaptation or a dedicated model.</li><li>Per region accuracy is monitored after release, since the caller mix shifts with campaigns.</li></ul><h2>How accent robustness is evaluated</h2><p>Accent coverage is established by disaggregating accuracy, because an aggregate figure conceals the regions that fail. Measure the following on live telephone audio:</p><ul><li>word error rate per region and per language background</li><li>error rate on the entity types a call actually depends on</li><li>gap between the strongest region and the weakest</li><li>escalation rate by region as a behavioural proxy</li><li>accuracy on second language speakers of the interface language</li></ul><h2>Limitations, risks and common failure modes</h2><ul><li>Test audio is recorded by the implementation team, who share one accent between them.</li><li>An aggregate accuracy figure conceals a region where the service is unusable.</li><li>Acoustic adaptation for one region degrades recognition in another without anyone checking.</li><li>Personal names and place names carry the highest error rates and the greatest consequence.</li></ul>",
