    "lede": "A sequence that changes according to what the learner has already demonstrated, rather than one fixed order for everyone.",
    "html": "<h2>What is an adaptive learning path?</h2><p>An adaptive learning path is a sequence of modules that changes according to what a learner has already demonstrated. Instead of one fixed order for everyone, the next module is selected from the assessment evidence collected so far. The path a learner took becomes part of their training record.</p><p class=\"gt-alias\"><b>Also called:</b> Adaptive pathway, Personalised learning path</p><h2>Why sequence is the part AI actually changes</h2><p>Mandatory training is resented because most of it addresses a learner who does not exist, someone with no prior knowledge at all. An adaptive path removes material a learner has already evidenced, which reduces time away from the desk without reducing coverage. The complication in a regulated setting is that an auditor asks what each person was required to complete. A path that adapts without recording why it adapted leaves a gap in the evidence.</p><ul><li>Learners disengage from material they have already demonstrated they know.</li><li>Shortening a path for one learner must not shorten the obligation on the record.</li><li>Adaptation concentrates the remaining time on the sections a learner answered poorly.</li><li>Every branch taken has to be explainable to someone examining the record later.</li></ul><h2>How an adaptive learning path works</h2><ul><li>Prior evidence is drawn from earlier assessments and from the completion history held per learner.</li><li>Each module declares the competencies it covers and the evidence that would waive it.</li><li>A diagnostic assessment establishes what the learner can already demonstrate.</li><li>The next module is selected from the gap between the role requirement and the evidence held.</li><li>Waivers and branches are written to the record with the rule that produced them.</li></ul><h2>How an adaptive learning path is evaluated</h2><p>An adaptive path is judged on whether it reaches the same coverage in less time. Examine the following across a complete cohort rather than a pilot group:</p><ul><li>median time to completion against a fixed sequence</li><li>assessment performance of learners whose path was shortened</li><li>proportion of waivers supported by retrievable prior evidence</li><li>coverage of required competencies at the end of each path</li><li>variance in path length within a single role</li></ul><h2>Limitations, risks and common failure modes</h2><ul><li>A waiver granted on stale evidence records training that never happened.</li><li>Adaptation is frequently driven by click behaviour rather than by demonstrated competence.</li><li>Learners on shortened paths miss changes introduced since their earlier assessment.</li><li>Regulators ask for the standard sequence, which an adaptive system may no longer hold.</li></ul>",
