    "lede": "Assessing candidates for a programme, where fairness and consistency are examined as closely as the outcome.",
    "html": "<h2>What is admissions evaluation?</h2><p>Admissions evaluation is the assessment of candidates against the criteria a programme has published for entry. It covers the whole cycle, from how an application is read to how the final decision is recorded. Fairness and consistency between reviewers are examined as closely as the outcome itself.</p><p class=\"gt-alias\"><b>Also called:</b> Admissions assessment</p><h2>Why an admissions decision is judged twice</h2><p>Every unsuccessful candidate receives a decision that affects a year of their life, and a proportion of them will ask how it was reached. An admissions office that cannot show the standard applied, and show that it was applied the same way to the applicant in position four hundred as to the applicant in position four, has no answer. Volume is what makes this difficult, since the later a reviewer reads, the less like the earlier reviewer they become. Consistency, therefore, is an engineering problem before it is a policy one.</p><ul><li>Admissions cycles concentrate thousands of judgements into a few weeks.</li><li>The published criteria become a commitment the organisation has to honour.</li><li>Reviewer fatigue changes the standard applied without anyone intending it.</li><li>Candidates compare notes, so inconsistency between decisions becomes visible.</li></ul><h2>How admissions evaluation works</h2><ul><li>Entry criteria are agreed and weighted before the application window opens.</li><li>Each application is parsed into the dimensions the criteria name.</li><li>Dimensions are scored independently, with the supporting passage attached to each score.</li><li>Scores are aggregated and compared against the threshold for the cohort.</li><li>Borderline and low confidence cases are routed to a named human reviewer.</li><li>Each score is retained with its author and every revision, for as long as the appeal window lasts.</li></ul><h2>How admissions evaluation is evaluated</h2><p>Admissions evaluation is judged on whether the same application would receive the same outcome in different hands. Examine the cycle on completed applications rather than on a curated set:</p><ul><li>agreement between independent reviewers on the same application</li><li>stability of scores from the first week of reading to the last</li><li>outcome rates across groups where adverse impact would carry legal consequence</li><li>share of decisions with evidence linked to every dimension</li><li>proportion of borderline cases escalated to a person</li></ul><h2>Limitations, risks and common failure modes</h2><ul><li>Criteria published to candidates often differ from the criteria reviewers actually apply.</li><li>Interview scores are frequently contaminated by the written file the panel has already read.</li><li>A late surge in applications compresses reading time and widens the variance in scoring.</li><li>Appeals arrive months later, by which time the reviewer has left the organisation.</li></ul>",
