    "lede": "A materially different outcome rate for a protected group, which carries legal consequence as well as reputational.",
    "html": "<h2>What is adverse impact?</h2><p>Adverse impact is a materially different rate of favourable outcomes for a protected group, arising from a process that makes no reference to that group. It is established statistically from the outcomes themselves rather than from the intent behind the process. In several jurisdictions it is actionable whether or not anyone intended it.</p><p class=\"gt-alias\"><b>Also called:</b> Disparate impact</p><h2>Why intent is irrelevant to an adverse impact finding</h2><p>The cost of discovering adverse impact late is rarely the remediation itself. It is the period between deployment and discovery, during which every decision taken is open to challenge, and the organisation has no defence prepared. Regulators and claimants work backwards from outcome data that the organisation already holds, so the finding is reconstructed from the evidence rather than argued from principle. An organisation that tests for adverse impact itself controls when it learns the answer.</p><ul><li>Adverse impact is measured on outcomes, so good intentions carry no weight.</li><li>A proxy variable can reproduce a protected characteristic the model never saw.</li><li>The exposure accumulates with every decision taken before the test is performed.</li><li>Self-testing converts a legal surprise into a managed engineering decision.</li></ul><h2>How adverse impact works</h2><ul><li>Protected groups relevant to the jurisdiction and the decision are identified.</li><li>Favourable outcome rates are calculated for each group against the reference group.</li><li>Ratios are compared against the applicable standard, commonly four-fifths in United States practice.</li><li>Any disparity found is traced to the dimension or the feature producing it.</li><li>The dimension is reweighted or removed, and the test is then repeated.</li><li>Results are recorded with the date and the cohort tested.</li></ul><h2>How adverse impact is evaluated</h2><p>Adverse impact is established by comparison, so the test is only as good as the groups and the window chosen. Examine the following on the full decided population:</p><ul><li>selection rate for each group against the highest-scoring reference group</li><li>the four-fifths ratio, with the count behind each rate</li><li>statistical significance given the size of the smallest group</li><li>disparity attributable to each individual dimension of the rubric</li><li>change in each ratio after a rubric or threshold revision</li></ul><h2>Limitations, risks and common failure modes</h2><ul><li>Small group sizes produce ratios that move sharply on a handful of decisions.</li><li>A proxy such as postcode or school reintroduces a characteristic that was removed.</li><li>Testing only at the final outcome hides a disparity that a single dimension creates.</li><li>Groups are defined differently by each regulator, so one test does not satisfy all of them.</li></ul>",
