    "lede": "Automation that decides the next step from the state it observes rather than replaying a fixed script.",
    "html": "<h2>What is agentic automation?</h2><p>Agentic automation decides its next step from the state it observes, rather than replaying a sequence recorded in advance. A scripted automation stops at the first unexpected screen, where an agentic one reads what is in front of it and chooses. Because the decision is taken at execution time, the record of what was chosen becomes part of the deliverable.</p><p class=\"gt-alias\"><b>Also called:</b> Agentic process automation</p><h2>What agentic automation claims that scripted automation cannot</h2><p>Most operations teams meet this phrase while holding a budget line that still says robotic process automation, and the two are frequently treated as the same purchase. The difference appears at the first unexpected screen, since a recorded script halts there and an agent proceeds. That capability is worth having and it transfers the hard question from reliability to accountability. An estate that chooses its own steps has to record every one of them.</p><ul><li>An agent selects its next action, which a recorded script never does.</li><li>The capability removes the brittleness that interface changes introduce.</li><li>Decisions taken at execution time cannot be reviewed from a design document.</li><li>Accountability becomes the harder engineering problem once scripting stops being one.</li></ul><h2>How agentic automation works</h2><ul><li>The task is expressed as an outcome with the permitted actions enumerated.</li><li>The agent observes the current state of each system it may address.</li><li>It selects the next action from that state and from the outcome it was given.</li><li>Every action is recorded with its inputs before the effect is committed.</li><li>Steps that cannot be reversed are gated behind a named approver.</li><li>Completion is verified against the outcome rather than against a step count.</li></ul><h2>How agentic automation is evaluated</h2><p>Agentic automation is judged on completion without supervision and on what the record supports afterwards. Examine the following across live volume rather than a pilot sample:</p><ul><li>share of tasks completed without a person intervening</li><li>actions taken per task against the minimum the task requires</li><li>proportion of completed tasks whose record reconstructs every action</li><li>frequency of actions attempted outside the permitted set</li><li>recovery rate after an unexpected application state</li></ul><h2>Limitations, risks and common failure modes</h2><ul><li>Teams procure agentic automation and then constrain it into a fixed script.</li><li>Permitted action sets are drafted once and never revisited after an integration changes.</li><li>An agent that chooses well on average still produces outliers nobody predicted.</li><li>Pilot environments lack the stale data that causes an agent to choose badly.</li></ul>",
