    "slug": "ai-audit-trail",
    "term": "AI audit trail",
    "set": "governance",
    "txt": "ai audit trail the complete record of what a system did, on what evidence, and who authorised it, retained for the period the decision can be questioned. evidence governance",
    "def": "The complete record of what a system did, on what evidence, and who authorised it, retained for the period the decision can be questioned.",
    "capability": {
      "label": "Governance",
      "href": "/engineering/ai-governance/"
    },
    "lede": "The complete record of what a system did, on what evidence, and who authorised it, retained for the period the decision can be questioned.",
    "html": "<h2>What is an AI audit trail?</h2><p>An AI audit trail is the complete record of what a system did on a given occasion and the evidence it acted on. Each step also names the authority under which it was taken, and the record is retained for as long as the decision remains open to challenge. The trail is assembled while the work happens, since none of it can be recovered afterwards.</p><p class=\"gt-alias\"><b>Also called:</b> Audit trail, Audit log</p><h2>Why the trail is the only version of events that survives</h2><p>An auditor does not arrive with a question about model architecture. They arrive with one case and a date, then ask to see what the system had in front of it at that moment. The organisation either produces the inputs at the version actually read together with the approval that followed, or it offers a recollection. Only one of those two things counts as evidence.</p><ul><li>A trail written after the event is a reconstruction, which carries no weight.</li><li>The record has to name the authority under which each step was taken.</li><li>Retention is governed by how long the decision stays open to challenge.</li><li>An incomplete trail converts a defensible decision into an unprovable one.</li></ul><h2>How an AI audit trail works</h2><ul><li>Every inbound request is recorded with its originator and a timestamp.</li><li>Each source document is pinned at the version the system actually read.</li><li>The model version and the policy in force are stamped onto each entry.</li><li>Tool calls and their returned values are stored alongside the output they produced.</li><li>Human approvals and overrides are appended with the identity of the approver.</li><li>Entries are written to storage that permits addition without amendment.</li></ul><h2>How an AI audit trail is evaluated</h2><p>An audit trail is assessed by attempting the reconstruction it exists to support. Examine the following on cases selected by someone outside the team:</p><ul><li>elapsed time to produce a complete account of one case</li><li>proportion of entries with the source version still retrievable</li><li>share of outputs whose tool calls were recorded in full</li><li>completeness of the approval chain including every override</li><li>retention period against the longest applicable challenge window</li></ul><h2>Limitations, risks and common failure modes</h2><ul><li>Logs are configured for engineering diagnostics rather than for a regulator.</li><li>Source documents are amended in place, so the version read is lost.</li><li>Timestamps are recorded in local time across systems in several jurisdictions.</li><li>Storage lifecycle rules delete entries before the appeal window has closed.</li><li>Export strips the links between an output and the evidence beneath it.</li></ul>",
    "related": [
      "decision-record",
      "append-only-log",
      "deterministic-replay",
      "tamper-evidence",
      "reasoning-trace",
      "run-ledger"
    ],
    "metaTitle": "AI audit trail: what a regulator asks for",
    "metaDescription": "An AI audit trail records what a system did and the evidence it acted on. What must be captured while the work happens for it to hold later."
