    "slug": "ai-risk-register",
    "term": "AI risk register",
    "set": "governance",
    "txt": "ai risk register the catalogue of identified ai risks, each with a named owner and a stated control, maintained as a live document. regulation governance",
    "def": "The catalogue of identified AI risks, each with a named owner and a stated control, maintained as a live document.",
    "capability": {
      "label": "Governance",
      "href": "/engineering/ai-governance/"
    },
    "lede": "The catalogue of identified AI risks, each with a named owner and a stated control, maintained as a live document.",
    "html": "<h2>What is an AI risk register?</h2><p>An AI risk register is the catalogue of AI risks an organisation has identified, each with a named owner and the control that addresses it. It is maintained as a live document rather than produced once for a committee pack. Every entry carries a current assessment and the date on which that assessment was made.</p><p class=\"gt-alias\"><b>Also called:</b> Risk register</p><h2>Why a register earns its place between committee meetings</h2><p>What a board reviews is rarely the model itself. It is a page naming the risks someone has accepted on the organisation's behalf and the control holding each one. A register assembled the week before that meeting describes only the week before that meeting. Its worth lies in whether an entry moved when the system moved.</p><ul><li>A risk without a named owner is an observation rather than a risk.</li><li>The register is where an accepted risk becomes a recorded decision.</li><li>Entries that never change indicate a register maintained for presentation.</li><li>Residual risk, once stated, tells a board what it has agreed to carry.</li><li>A register the organisation maintains itself sets the agenda for an inspection.</li></ul><h2>How an AI risk register works</h2><ul><li>Each AI system in use is entered with its purpose and its affected population.</li><li>Risks are described as events with a consequence rather than as themes.</li><li>Likelihood and impact are assessed against a scale the organisation has published.</li><li>An owner with authority over the system is named against every entry.</li><li>Each risk is paired with a control and the evidence that the control operates.</li><li>Entries are reassessed on a stated cadence and whenever the system changes.</li></ul><h2>How an AI risk register is evaluated</h2><p>A register is judged on whether it reflects the systems currently in production. Test the following against what is actually deployed:</p><ul><li>share of deployed systems with a register entry</li><li>proportion of entries with an owner who holds real authority</li><li>age of the most recent assessment per entry</li><li>number of controls with evidence of operation attached</li><li>count of entries closed without a stated justification</li></ul><h2>Limitations, risks and common failure modes</h2><ul><li>Registers are populated at a programme launch and then left to age.</li><li>Risks are written broadly enough that no control can be tested against them.</li><li>Ownership is assigned to a function rather than to a person who can act.</li><li>Controls are described as intentions with no evidence that they operate.</li><li>The register omits systems procured by a business unit without central review.</li></ul>",
