    "slug": "applicant-experience",
    "term": "Applicant experience",
    "set": "loupe",
    "txt": "applicant experience how the process feels to the person completing it, which most intake programmes measure last or not at all. operating model loupe",
    "def": "How the process feels to the person completing it, which most intake programmes measure last or not at all.",
    "capability": {
      "label": "Loupe",
      "href": "/pyzo/loupe/"
    },
    "lede": "How the process feels to the person completing it, which most intake programmes measure last or not at all.",
    "html": "<h2>What is applicant experience?</h2><p>Applicant experience is the quality of the process as the person completing a form encounters it. It covers how long the form takes and how much of it has to be redone after a rejection. In regulated intake it is the variable most closely connected to whether a submission arrives at all.</p><h2>Why the applicant's side of intake is measured last</h2><p>Nobody inside an intake function is held to account for how the form felt. Case officers are measured on throughput and on backlog, where the applicant's time is spent outside the organisation and therefore outside its reporting. The consequence is a process that looks efficient internally while losing a large share of its applicants before submission. Once completion rates enter the same report as throughput, the design conversation changes.</p><ul><li>Abandonment happens outside the organisation, so it leaves no internal trace.</li><li>A form taking four evenings to complete selects for persistence rather than merit.</li><li>Correction cycles consume applicant time that no internal metric ever records.</li><li>Completion rate is the single number predicting whether volume targets are reached.</li></ul><h2>How applicant experience works</h2><ul><li>The journey is instrumented at every field, so lost attention becomes visible.</li><li>Completion time is measured per section against the time that section was expected to take.</li><li>Validation is moved to the point of entry so errors surface while context is fresh.</li><li>Help text is written from the questions applicants actually asked a helpline.</li><li>Partial progress is preserved, allowing an applicant to return without loss.</li><li>Each release is measured against the previous cohort on completion rather than on opinion.</li></ul><h2>How applicant experience is evaluated</h2><p>Applicant experience is measured on what applicants did rather than on what a survey reports. Examine the following across a full intake cycle:</p><ul><li>completion rate from first field to submission</li><li>median time to complete each section of the form</li><li>share of submissions requiring a correction after submission</li><li>drop-off position within the form, reported per section</li><li>proportion of applicants contacting the helpline during completion</li></ul><h2>Limitations, risks and common failure modes</h2><ul><li>Satisfaction surveys reach the applicants who finished, missing everyone who gave up.</li><li>Internal usability testing is performed by staff who already know the policy.</li><li>Mobile completion is treated as an edge case where it is the majority.</li><li>A form is lengthened by one field each cycle while nobody owns the total length.</li></ul>",
