    "lede": "Adding the context a passage needs to be understood on its own, such as its document title and section path, before it is indexed.",
    "html": "<h2>What is chunk enrichment?</h2><p>Chunk enrichment adds context to each passage before it is indexed, so the passage can be understood without the document around it. The additions typically include the document title, the section heading path, key dates and a short statement of what the surrounding document is about. Both the embedding and the keyword index then see a passage that carries its own context instead of an isolated fragment.</p><p class=\"gt-alias\"><b>Also called:</b> Contextual chunk enrichment, Contextual retrieval</p><h2>Why a passage separated from its document loses its meaning</h2><p>Chunking divides documents into passages that fit the index, and many of those passages make sense only in place. A paragraph that begins with this limit applies to all accounts says nothing about which product or which policy it belongs to. Retrieval cannot match it to a question about that product, and the model cannot cite it accurately. Enrichment restores the missing context at the moment of indexing, so each passage is findable and interpretable on its own.</p><ul><li>Passages that rely on earlier text become unfindable once separated from it.</li><li>Section paths distinguish identical wording used in different policies.</li><li>Enriched passages give the model enough context to cite them correctly.</li><li>The enrichment is applied once at indexing and costs nothing at query time.</li></ul><h2>How chunk enrichment works</h2><ul><li>Each passage is paired with its document title and heading path.</li><li>A model writes a short statement placing the passage within the document.</li><li>Key entities and dates from the wider document are attached where relevant.</li><li>The enriched text is embedded and indexed in place of the bare passage.</li><li>The original passage text is kept separately for display and citation.</li><li>Enrichment is regenerated whenever the source document changes.</li></ul><h2>How chunk enrichment is evaluated</h2><p>Chunk enrichment is evaluated by indexing the same corpus with and without it. Measure the following on labelled questions:</p><ul><li>hit rate with enriched passages against bare passages</li><li>retrieval accuracy on questions that name a product or policy</li><li>share of enrichment statements supported by the source document</li><li>growth in index size caused by the added text</li><li>time added to ingestion per document</li></ul><h2>Limitations, risks and common failure modes</h2><ul><li>Generated context introduces claims the document does not make.</li><li>Long enrichment text outweighs the passage it was meant to describe.</li><li>Enrichment is not refreshed when the surrounding document changes.</li><li>The enriched text is shown to readers in place of the original passage.</li></ul>",
    "related": [
      "chunking",
      "document-summarisation",
      "metadata-extraction",
      "embedding",
      "hybrid-search",
      "document-ingestion"
    ],
    "metaTitle": "Chunk enrichment: giving each passage its context",
    "metaDescription": "Chunk enrichment adds the surrounding context a passage needs to be understood on its own, such as its document and section. How the added context improves retrieval accuracy."
