    "slug": "field-level-validation",
    "term": "Field-level validation",
    "set": "loupe",
    "txt": "field-level validation checking an entry at the field where it was made, so the error surfaces to the person who can correct it. validation loupe",
    "def": "Checking an entry at the field where it was made, so the error surfaces to the person who can correct it.",
    "capability": {
      "label": "Loupe",
      "href": "/pyzo/loupe/"
    },
    "lede": "Checking an entry at the field where it was made, so the error surfaces to the person who can correct it.",
    "html": "<h2>What is field-level validation?</h2><p>Field-level validation is the checking of a single entry at the field where it was made. The check is applied while the applicant is still present and the context still fresh, so a correction costs seconds. The same error found at assessment costs a letter and a wait of several weeks.</p><p class=\"gt-alias\"><b>Also called:</b> Inline validation</p><h2>Why the distance between error and discovery is the whole cost</h2><p>The interval between an applicant making a mistake and learning of it is the real cost of a validation design. At the field it is immediate and costs a keystroke. At assessment the same mistake costs six weeks and a case officer's afternoon. Field-level validation moves the check to the moment at which correction is almost free, which is its entire contribution.</p><ul><li>The applicant holds the correct value at entry and rarely retains it later.</li><li>An error corrected at the field never enters the assessment queue.</li><li>Immediate feedback teaches the applicant what the field actually wants.</li><li>Checks placed at the field reduce the volume that cross-field rules must handle.</li></ul><h2>How field-level validation works</h2><ul><li>Each field declares its permitted format together with the rule behind it.</li><li>The check is evaluated as the applicant leaves the field.</li><li>The message names what is wrong and what a valid entry looks like.</li><li>Checks requiring a lookup against an external register are performed asynchronously.</li><li>Failures are recorded with a reason code even where the applicant corrects them.</li><li>Rule placement is reviewed against where errors are actually being caught.</li></ul><h2>How field-level validation is evaluated</h2><p>Field-level validation is measured by how many errors it removes before submission. Examine the following across completed forms:</p><ul><li>share of errors detected at the field rather than at assessment</li><li>proportion of flagged errors corrected before the applicant proceeds</li><li>false rejection rate on entries that were in fact valid</li><li>time between the check firing and the applicant resolving it</li><li>abandonment rate immediately after a validation failure</li></ul><h2>Limitations, risks and common failure modes</h2><ul><li>Over-strict formats reject valid names and addresses from outside the designer's experience.</li><li>A check firing on every keystroke reads as hostile to the applicant.</li><li>Messages describe the rule in policy language the applicant cannot interpret.</li><li>Client-side checks are absent on the server, so a crafted submission bypasses them.</li></ul>",
    "related": [
      "cross-field-validation",
      "validation-at-the-point-of-entry",
      "attachment-validation",
      "business-rule-validation",
      "error-reason-code",
      "mandatory-field-coverage"
    ],
    "metaTitle": "Field-level validation: caught at entry",
    "metaDescription": "Field-level validation checks an entry at the field where it was made, so the error reaches the person who can correct it. How rules are placed and tested."
