    "slug": "rework-rate",
    "term": "Rework rate",
    "set": "loupe",
    "txt": "rework rate the share of submissions that have to go back, and the cost that follows each return. measurement loupe",
    "def": "The share of submissions that have to go back, and the cost that follows each return.",
    "capability": {
      "label": "Loupe",
      "href": "/pyzo/loupe/"
    },
    "lede": "The share of submissions that have to go back, and the cost that follows each return.",
    "html": "<h2>What is the rework rate?</h2><p>The rework rate is the proportion of submissions that have to be returned for correction. It is counted on first-pass submissions and attributed to the field that caused the return, which is what makes it actionable. A rework rate reported at the level of the form tells an organisation something is wrong without indicating what.</p><p class=\"gt-alias\"><b>Also called:</b> Return rate</p><h2>Why rework is the most expensive number nobody owns</h2><p>The cost of a returned submission falls in several places at once, which is why it is so rarely totalled. A reviewer reads the same file twice while a support desk handles the query that follows. The applicant waits, and the service commitment the organisation published is missed. Each of those costs belongs to a different function, so the rework rate reaches nobody with authority over the form producing it.</p><ul><li>Rework cost is distributed across functions, so no single budget shows it.</li><li>A rework rate attributed per field identifies the question to rewrite.</li><li>Returned submissions consume reviewer capacity that was planned for new ones.</li><li>Applicants who abandon after a return never appear in the rework figure.</li></ul><h2>How the rework rate works</h2><ul><li>First-pass submissions are distinguished from corrections at the point of receipt.</li><li>Each return is recorded against the specific fields that caused it.</li><li>The rate is computed per form version and per applicant segment.</li><li>Reviewer and support effort per return is estimated and attached to the rate.</li><li>Fields with the highest attributed rework are examined against their guidance.</li><li>The rate is remeasured after the form or its assistance is amended.</li></ul><h2>How the rework rate is evaluated</h2><p>Rework is read as a cost figure as well as a rate, which means the effort behind each return has to be attached to it. Examine the following per form version:</p><ul><li>first-pass rework rate with the field attribution behind it</li><li>reviewer minutes and support contacts per returned submission</li><li>abandonment following a return, counted separately from correction</li><li>change in the rate after each amendment to the form</li><li>share of returns raised for a reason the applicant could not anticipate</li></ul><h2>Limitations, risks and common failure modes</h2><ul><li>Corrections are counted as new submissions, which removes rework from the statistics.</li><li>Returns are attributed to the applicant's error rather than the question's wording.</li><li>The rate improves because reviewers stop returning files they should return.</li><li>Applicants lost after a return are recorded as withdrawals rather than as rework.</li></ul>",
    "related": [
      "first-pass-yield",
      "abandonment-rate",
      "containment-rate",
      "hit-rate",
      "queue-depth",
      "data-quality-score"
    ],
    "metaTitle": "Rework rate: the cost of a form that fails",
    "metaDescription": "The rework rate is the share of submissions returned for correction, and the cost that follows each return. How it is attributed to an individual field."
