    "lede": "Coordinating steps across several systems, with the dependencies and the failure handling defined.",
    "html": "<h2>What is workflow orchestration?</h2><p>Workflow orchestration is the coordination of steps across several systems under a single definition. The definition states the order of the steps along with the dependencies between them, naming what happens when one fails. Control belongs with the orchestrator rather than being distributed across the steps themselves.</p><h2>Why coordination has to live somewhere nameable</h2><p>Work that moves between systems through a chain of triggers has no single place where its state can be read. Each system knows its own part, so a case that stopped between two of them stops silently and is found by a customer rather than by a report. Orchestration concentrates that knowledge in one definition, which is what makes approvals and reversal expressible at all. The moment this becomes obvious is the first failure in the middle of a chain.</p><ul><li>A chain of triggers leaves no single place to read a case's state.</li><li>Dependencies expressed once are enforceable, where convention is merely hoped for.</li><li>Approval and reversal need a definition that spans every system involved.</li><li>Attempt and timeout policy belong with the coordinator rather than each step.</li></ul><h2>How workflow orchestration works</h2><ul><li>The steps are declared with their dependencies in one workflow definition.</li><li>Each step states its inputs alongside the outcome that marks it complete.</li><li>The orchestrator persists the position of every case at each transition.</li><li>Failure handling is declared per step rather than left to the step's own code.</li><li>Approvals are expressed as steps that wait for a named person.</li><li>Progress and exceptions are exposed on one view for the operations team.</li></ul><h2>How workflow orchestration is evaluated</h2><p>Workflow orchestration is assessed on what it can tell you about work in flight. Examine the following across live cases:</p><ul><li>share of cases whose current position is readable from one place</li><li>cases stalled between two systems without an exception raised</li><li>elapsed time per step against the definition's expectation</li><li>proportion of steps with declared failure handling</li><li>changes to the definition deployed without a version record</li></ul><h2>Limitations, risks and common failure modes</h2><ul><li>Orchestration logic migrates into the steps, so the definition stops being authoritative.</li><li>A long waiting step holds a worker that other cases need.</li><li>Timeouts are set per step while no limit covers the case overall.</li><li>A definition change applies to cases already in flight under the previous version.</li><li>The operations view shows failures and omits cases quietly waiting.</li></ul>",
