    "slug": "write-back",
    "term": "Write-back",
    "set": "compass",
    "txt": "write-back returning a result into the system of record, which is where an answering layer takes on real consequence. action compass",
    "def": "Returning a result into the system of record, which is where an answering layer takes on real consequence.",
    "capability": {
      "label": "Compass",
      "href": "/pyzo/compass/"
    },
    "lede": "Returning a result into the system of record, which is where an answering layer takes on real consequence.",
    "html": "<h2>What is write-back?</h2><p>Write-back is the return of a result produced by an AI layer into the system of record that owns the fact. Reading from a record leaves it unchanged, where a write becomes part of the organisation's authoritative history. It therefore passes through the same validation and the same approval as any other change to that record.</p><h2>Why a write commits the organisation in a way a read never does</h2><p>Every control argument about an answering layer changes character at the first write. A wrong answer can be corrected by the person reading it, where a wrong write has already entered a case file other processes read from. The integration itself is seldom the difficult part of this. Deciding which results may be written without a person confirming them is harder, as is proving afterwards that each write happened exactly once.</p><ul><li>A write enters the authoritative history other processes depend upon.</li><li>Correction after a write requires a reversal that is itself auditable.</li><li>Approval has to be designed per write type rather than applied uniformly.</li><li>Without idempotency a retry duplicates the record a user will later query.</li></ul><h2>How write-back works</h2><ul><li>The write types permitted are enumerated, each with the record it touches.</li><li>Field-level validation is applied before the write is proposed.</li><li>Writes above a defined consequence are held for a named approver.</li><li>Each write carries an idempotency key so a retry cannot duplicate it.</li><li>The write is executed through the record system's own interface and validation.</li><li>The executed write is recorded against its approver and the question that produced it.</li></ul><h2>How write-back is evaluated</h2><p>Write-back is evaluated on correctness and on reversibility rather than on throughput. Measure the following across writes already executed:</p><ul><li>duplicate write rate under retry and timeout conditions</li><li>proportion of consequential writes carrying a named approver</li><li>field-level validation failure rate before execution</li><li>elapsed time to reverse an incorrect write once identified</li><li>completeness of the link from each write to its originating question</li></ul><h2>Limitations, risks and common failure modes</h2><ul><li>Retries without an idempotency key create duplicate records nobody reconciles.</li><li>Approval is configured in the test environment and omitted in production.</li><li>A write bypasses the record system's validation by going directly to its database.</li><li>Reversal procedures are documented and never rehearsed until an incident requires one.</li></ul>",
