    "slug": "zero-data-retention",
    "term": "Zero data retention",
    "set": "governance",
    "txt": "zero data retention a commitment that inputs and outputs are not stored after a request completes. security governance",
    "def": "A commitment that inputs and outputs are not stored after a request completes.",
    "capability": {
      "label": "Governance",
      "href": "/engineering/ai-governance/"
    },
    "lede": "A commitment that inputs and outputs are not stored after a request completes.",
    "html": "<h2>What is zero data retention?</h2><p>Zero data retention is a commitment that the inputs and outputs of a request are not stored once the request completes. It governs the model provider's side of the boundary, where content exists only for the duration of inference. The commitment is a configuration with contractual backing, which is what makes it verifiable rather than merely reassuring.</p><p class=\"gt-alias\"><b>Also called:</b> ZDR</p><h2>Why zero retention answers only half of the data question</h2><p>Zero retention is frequently read as a complete answer to data protection, when it addresses one specific portion of the problem. It governs what the provider keeps, and says nothing about the traces and evaluation stores a deploying organisation maintains on its own side. Those are usually where content actually accumulates, under a retention policy nobody has reviewed. A procurement team confirming zero retention upstream while never examining its own logging has moved the exposure rather than removed it.</p><ul><li>The commitment removes a class of exposure at the provider's end of the path.</li><li>Abuse monitoring is the usual exception, so the terms have to be read closely.</li><li>Retention on the deploying side is where content accumulates in practice.</li><li>A verifiable setting is stronger evidence than an assurance in a proposal.</li></ul><h2>How zero data retention works</h2><ul><li>The retention setting is applied at the endpoint and recorded in the configuration.</li><li>Contractual terms confirming the commitment are obtained before the first request.</li><li>Exceptions such as abuse monitoring are identified and assessed explicitly.</li><li>The organisation's own logging and tracing retention is set deliberately by policy.</li><li>Evaluation and monitoring stores are brought within the same retention schedule.</li><li>The configuration is reverified after every provider or endpoint change.</li></ul><h2>How zero data retention is evaluated</h2><p>The commitment covers one end of the path, so verification has to examine both. Confirm the following across the whole request path:</p><ul><li>retention setting at the endpoint, confirmed from the provider's configuration</li><li>contractual term covering retention and any stated exception</li><li>retention period on prompt content inside the organisation's own traces</li><li>content held in evaluation and monitoring stores, with its retention</li><li>deletion evidence for content captured before the setting was applied</li></ul><h2>Limitations, risks and common failure modes</h2><ul><li>Zero retention is agreed upstream while internal traces keep prompts indefinitely.</li><li>An abuse monitoring exception retains content the organisation assumed was discarded.</li><li>A fallback provider is called during an outage under entirely different terms.</li><li>The setting is applied to one endpoint and omitted on another in the same deployment.</li><li>Content captured before the commitment was in force is never deleted.</li></ul>",
    "related": [
      "data-residency",
      "personal-data-masking",
      "private-model-endpoint",
      "tenancy-isolation",
      "customer-managed-keys",
      "deny-by-default-egress"
    ],
    "metaTitle": "Zero data retention: what the commitment covers",
